Reason:
The logic allows the same configuration type (`serviceConfigType`) to be added multiple times to the `blueprintConfigRequest` if it exists in `mapClusterConfigurations`. This can lead to inconsistencies and conflicts in the cluster configuration, especially when multiple services share the same configuration type.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
Collection<String> encounteredConfigTypes = new HashSet<String>();
if (encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)) {
  // skip handling of cluster-env here
  if (!serviceConfigType.equals("cluster-env")) {
    if (mapClusterConfigurations.containsKey(serviceConfigType)) {
      blueprintConfigRequest.addConfigElement(serviceConfigType,
        mapClusterConfigurations.get(serviceConfigType),
        mapClusterAttributes.get(serviceConfigType));
    }
  }
}
```
Reason:
The addition of the `encounteredConfigTypes` set ensures that each configuration type is only added once to the `blueprintConfigRequest`. By using `encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)`, it checks if the configuration type has already been encountered, preventing duplicates and thus resolving the potential conflicts and inconsistencies in the cluster configuration.

